# pnpm audit ignore list — PRODUCTION dependency tree
#
# Format mirrors .pip-audit-ignore: one GHSA per line, justification in the
# comment(s) above it, and a `# expires:` date governing the run of IDs beneath.
#
# THIS FILE IS THE SOURCE OF TRUTH. pnpm itself reads the ID list from
# package.json -> pnpm.auditConfig.ignoreGhsas; scripts/check-audit-ignore.py
# fails if the two ever disagree, so a suppression cannot be added to the
# machine-readable list without the justification and expiry that live here.
#
# Rating key (same as .pip-audit-ignore):
#   ACCEPT  -- not exploitable in Morse's context
#   MONITOR -- unclear exploitability, track for future review
#
# Re-verification rule: an entry earns its place only while `pnpm audit --prod`
# still reports it. A stale entry is worse than no entry — it silently
# re-suppresses the finding if the dependency ever regresses. Both that rule
# and the expiry are ENFORCED by scripts/check-audit-ignore.py.
#
# Scope note: this list applies to the PRODUCTION tree only, which is what the
# `pnpm-audit-prod` CI job gates. Dev/build-tooling advisories are reported
# weekly by dependency-audit.yml and are deliberately NOT suppressible here —
# a build tool running in CI with real credentials is a supply-chain surface,
# not a non-issue.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# image-size — 2 x parser denial-of-service. NO UPSTREAM FIX EXISTS.
#
# `pnpm audit` reports "Patched versions: <0.0.0" for both: every published
# version <=2.0.2 is vulnerable and no fixed release exists at any version, so
# an override cannot clear these. The only alternatives were to remove the
# dependency (it is transitive — nothing in this repo declares it directly) or
# to block all merges on an advisory with no remedy.
#
# ACCEPT rationale: both are denial-of-service in image PARSERS (ICNS, JXL,
# HEIF). Morse does not parse untrusted images anywhere on a request path —
# image-size reaches the production tree through the docs/asset toolchain, not
# through anything that handles user uploads or request bodies. The exploit
# requires feeding a crafted image to the parser, which no Morse code path
# does.
#
# Founder accepted this exception explicitly, in-session, on 2026-08-07.
#
# Re-review must actually re-judge it, not extend the date: check whether an
# upstream fix has shipped (`pnpm why image-size`, then the advisory pages) and
# whether Morse has since grown any path that parses user-supplied images.
# expires: 2026-11-07
GHSA-w3rx-r6r6-pgpr
GHSA-5p2g-fcmc-qvqq

